s  where maybe the two robots collide with  each other  so that is not allowed because that  violates our hard constraint  so we're going to pick out a subset of  this space which is the subset  satisfying this hard constraint h  so they're in the language of that  specification we're going to call those  the improvisations or the set  i now that set also contains things that  are safe maybe they satisfy the hard  constraint  but they violate the soft constraints so  like here this path  visits this circle twice so we're going  to call  that an inadmissible improvisation and  we'll have a further subset  a of admissible improvisations these are  the ones that satisfy both the heart and  the soft constraint  so here for example this plan visits all  four circles and it doesn't visit any of  them twice  so that would be an admissible  improvisation and that's in this set a  right so keeping this picture in mind of  these different sets  now we can set up the definition of a  control improvisation problem  so given uh the hard and soft constraint  this length bound n  and a couple of other parameters so  we're going to have an error probability  epsilon  which is going to say basically how  often you're allowed to violate the soft  constraint  and some probability bounds lambda and  rho that will be used to constrain how  much randomness there is  we'll see in a second we'll say that a  distribution  over words is an improvising  distribution if it satisfies three  conditions  so the hard constraint says that if you  sample a word from this distribution  it has to satisfy this hard  specification h that is it has to be an  improvisation  with probability one the soft constraint  is similar it says if you sample a word  it has to be an admissible improvisation  so in this further subset a most of the  time  so at least you know one minus epsilon  probability so you can tune  how tight this constraint is by picking  epsilon  and finally to enforce randomness we'll  say t