 of surveillance  problem where the robot in the  bottom left is the one we're controlling  it has to visit these four  circles while avoiding collisions with  an adversary which is the blue  dashed line and just to show you the  randomness in all four of these  simulations i've had the adversary move  in the same way  on the grid world and because of the  physics it's not moving in exactly the  same way each time  but it's ba it's making the same path  through the grid world and you'll see  that our improviser the black line is  responding in a very different way each  time  while still satisfying all of these  constraints that we see on the left  and here we were actually using a  randomness requirement saying make  row as small as possible while still  satisfying the other constraints  so in some sense this is the maximally  randomized policy  and our algorithm actually allows you to  compute this smallest feasible row  okay so this gives you an idea of the  problem definition  and now let me tell you a little bit  about uh the theory of control  improvisation  oops okay so the first question you  could ask is when can you  solve one of these problems and it turns  out that feasibility just requires these  sets i  and a to be large enough at least with  lambda equals zero which is what we're  talking about here  so in particular a ci instance with  lambda equals zero  is feasible if and only if these two  sets are sufficiently large  and the idea behind these inequalities  is basically that if these sets are too  small  you may be able to solve the problem but  not in a random enough way  so if there's only one way to solve the  constraints for instance  then you can't possibly have a random  strategy because you have to just  generate that one thing  that satisfies the constraints and so  the analysis basically shows you how you  need to trade off  the size of these sets versus the  epsilon and row that you're requiring  and where this proof basically comes out 