e  it's just now instead of the size of  these sets in a  we have this new notion of size which we  call the width  and the way that works is it the width  basically counts  the number of winning plays minimized  over all possible adversaries  so here's the definition what we say is  we maximize over all possible  strategies for us the improviser then we  minimize  over all strategies of the adversary  of this inner quantity and this notation  here is saying the probability of  getting a particular trace  w given the strategies of the two  players  so what we'll do is count what are all  the traces that have  non-zero probability given these two  strategies  and we want to make that as large as  possible and the adversary wants to make  it as small as possible  so this is what we call the width so the  width of x is the  basically the maximum number of plays  that we can ensure  are possible to play what regardless of  what the adversary does uh  so once you have this we get the same  kind of inequalities we had before  constructing an improviser turns out to  be a lot more involved than in the  non-reactive case that i described  before in particular it turns out that  the strategy cannot be memoryless even  if you have just like a safety  specification  where it's known you have memoryless  strategies normally  here the need to have to do it randomly  actually requ forces you in some way to  ha  to need some memory but the way our  strategy works is essentially does a  kind of random walk  remembering the widths so you construct  the game graph  here and the numbers that are being  shown in this example  are the widths so if you have the  winning states then you have one way to  win from there  and so basically if you minimize at the  adversary controlled states  and some at the states you control this  gives you a dynamic programming kind of  way to compute the width  so here we can work out that from the  initial state we have  a way to win at least four differe